1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,440
that the equilibrium prices of x and y

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:07,720
are just going to be equal to a over b times y bar over x bar.

3
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:10,560
And this is a PhD conclusion.

4
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:11,600
That is a PhD conclusion.

5
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:12,880
Now, what does this mean?

6
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:15,240
Well, it says that in the society that we just defined,

7
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:17,240
so this one, everyone's got an ethical preference

8
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:20,080
and an endowment, everyone's got the utility function,

9
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:22,600
then in equilibrium, the ratio of the price of x,

10
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:25,480
the price of y, remember, this is p sub x,

11
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:28,160
so it's not p times x, it's p sub x,

12
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:30,600
is going to be equal to a over b.

13
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:32,200
And again, so what does that mean?

14
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:33,600
Well, what's a?

15
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:35,520
A high a means what?

16
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:36,960
What does high a mean?

17
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:38,080
Like x more.

18
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:39,560
Yeah, people like x more.

19
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:41,840
What does high b mean?

20
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:43,400
People like y more.

21
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:46,040
What does a big y bar mean?

22
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:47,840
The world has a lot of y.

23
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:48,880
Lots of y.

24
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:50,560
What does a high x bar mean?

25
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:51,560
The world has lots of x.

26
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:52,640
The world has lots of x.

27
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:55,240
So this says that if people like x more,

28
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:56,640
then the price of x goes up.

29
00:00:56,640 --> 00:01:00,080
If people like y more, then the price of x relative to y

30
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:00,680
goes down.

31
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:04,520
If there's more y, then x is going to be more valuable.

32
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:08,080
If there's more x, then y will be more valuable.

33
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:12,240
And in particular, given these assumptions in this model,

34
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:15,800
this is exactly what's necessary to make the world stable.

35
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:20,040
If the price of x divided by y were higher than this,

36
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:25,760
then people would want to go and dump a ton of x

37
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:26,760
by y.

38
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:27,760
But there's the problem.

39
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:32,920
If everybody wants to dump x and buy y, can society do that?

40
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:33,760
No, of course not.

41
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:37,680
Society cannot collectively dump its x and get y

42
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:39,800
if that society is everything.

43
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:42,000
Of course, one country could go and dump its x in order

44
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:42,880
to get y.

45
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:46,960
But the universe of Asians cannot do that.

46
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:49,920
And so that's why this actually doesn't

47
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:51,520
contain some useful insight here.

48
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:54,000
OK.

49
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,240
All right.

50
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:57,360
And again, how is this different from this?

51
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:01,120
This is telling us what one individual does.

52
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:04,480
This is telling us what society does, given these assumptions.

53
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:09,000
You've actually been able to build a whole world out

54
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,080
of some very specific assumptions

55
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:13,280
about individual behavior.

56
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:15,400
Now, you may say, yeah, well, we build a world that

57
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:17,080
is nothing like the real world.

58
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:20,080
And in fact, it's such a narrow, stripped down world, who cares?

59
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:23,000
I know, it's an interesting homework problem anyway.

60
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,160
But also, as you might guess, one of the best ways

61
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:29,000
to start understanding more complex problems

62
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,680
is to start with a really simple problem

63
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:32,440
and then work your way up.

64
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:34,080
Robin Hanson was telling me that there's

65
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:36,520
a famous mathematician who published a book with just,

66
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:38,920
I think it was convenience, it was called How to Solve It.

67
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:40,840
Anyone heard of this book?

68
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:42,920
And it just contains a bunch of strategies

69
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:44,400
for solving problems.

70
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:46,840
And two of the top strategies are,

71
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:50,640
if you're stuck on a problem, A, try to solve a more general

72
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:52,280
version of the problem, or B, try

73
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:54,640
to solve a more specific version of the problem.

74
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:58,640
And both actually are useful strategies if you're stuck.

75
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:00,080
You can go either way, of course.

76
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:02,440
The more general one is a little bit more helpful

77
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:03,640
if you can pull it off.

78
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:05,000
Because if you prove the more general thing,

79
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,320
then you've automatically proved the more specific thing.

80
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:09,840
But on the other hand, doing the simpler problem

81
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:11,360
is probably easier.

82
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:14,480
So at least that's how I am.

83
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:17,600
So questions about this really simple economy

84
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:20,520
and how we now have a glorious PhD result?

85
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:26,160
So in that case, what if we made things more interesting

86
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:29,520
by allowing the individuals to be different from each other?

87
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:31,640
So everybody can have their own preferences.

88
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:33,800
Everybody can have their own endowments.

89
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:37,760
So at least we've got a real motive for trade.

90
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:39,400
Here, the whole point of prices is just

91
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:42,040
to convince people they don't want to trade,

92
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:43,960
which is a strange market.

93
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:45,200
But nobody in the market can convince people

94
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:47,200
that no one wants to participate in the markets.

95
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:49,880
It seems like a dumb thing to do,

96
00:03:49,880 --> 00:03:53,800
but it's implicit in the model.

97
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:57,120
Anyway, so what happens if we give everybody

98
00:03:57,120 --> 00:03:59,520
their own tastes and their own endowment?

99
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:03,280
In particular, just to keep things tractable,

100
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:06,360
why don't we keep the basic functional form of utility,

101
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:09,920
but then give every person his own preference parameters?

102
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:12,840
So everybody still has this log utility function.

103
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:17,120
But one person can have utilities 0.9 log x plus 0.1 log y.

104
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:23,120
Another one can have 0.03 log x plus 0.97 log y, and so on.

105
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:25,800
And then for the endowments, anybody can have anything.

106
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:28,160
So some people can have zero.

107
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:30,040
Some people can have a ton.

108
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:32,840
It's all OK.

109
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:35,600
But then, again, what happens is that instead of there

110
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:38,160
being a common x bar and y bar, now we

111
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:40,800
have an xi bar and a yi bar.

112
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:43,440
This indicates the endowment of person i,

113
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:45,480
endowment of x of person i, the endowment of y

114
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:47,520
of person i.

115
00:04:47,520 --> 00:04:48,520
Happy?

116
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:52,800
I don't feel completely alert at this time of the evening.

117
00:04:52,800 --> 00:04:53,800
All right.

118
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:56,520
So yeah, topology coming up.

119
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:59,280
All right, that's the easiest kind of topology.

120
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:00,800
All right.

121
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:02,840
OK, so what do we got?

122
00:05:02,840 --> 00:05:03,880
All right.

123
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:06,600
So as I said, so now the agents are actually

124
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:08,840
going to be making trades at a greater prices.

125
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:11,480
So people who like x a lot are going

126
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:12,680
to tend to be buying some x.

127
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:14,720
They're going to be buying x by selling y.

128
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:16,520
And people who don't start off with much x

129
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:21,040
are going to be tending to buy x with y.

130
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:22,880
So what we need to do is find not

131
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:26,200
the prices that will persuade every individual to stick

132
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:27,520
with what they started.

133
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:29,000
That's not necessary.

134
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,920
Any one individual can go and sell some x and buy some y.

135
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:33,920
But still, society cannot sell x.

136
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:36,240
And society cannot sell y.

137
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:38,960
So what we need then is to find the prices that

138
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:40,400
will induce aggregate consumption

139
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:42,040
to equal aggregate endowments.

140
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:43,620
So in other words, so in equilibrium,

141
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:45,080
what we need is that the summation

142
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:48,120
of all the consumption of x has to equal the summation

143
00:05:48,120 --> 00:05:51,760
of all the endowments of x.

144
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:53,480
See, so the xi's without the bars,

145
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:55,280
that's what people actually do.

146
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:59,080
And the xi bars, that's what people start with.

147
00:05:59,080 --> 00:06:02,360
But again, it's perfectly fine if any one individual

148
00:06:02,360 --> 00:06:04,280
doesn't consume what they started with.

149
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:06,360
And if you're not consuming what you started with,

150
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:08,720
what are you doing?

151
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:09,220
Selling.

152
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:10,240
Yeah, trading.

153
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:12,720
So if you are consuming more than you started with,

154
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:13,720
you bought some.

155
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:15,880
And if you're consuming less than you started with,

156
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:17,840
then you were selling some.

157
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:19,040
So what do we do?

158
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:20,720
Well, yeah, so we're going to add up

159
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:23,040
i equations for individual consumption of x

160
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:25,240
as a function of price of the initial endowments.

161
00:06:25,240 --> 00:06:28,800
And then we're going to impose constraints like this one.

162
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:30,920
And of course, a similar constraint for y.

163
00:06:30,920 --> 00:06:32,920
So what does this give us?

164
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:34,280
Well, I got it in the notes.

165
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:36,080
So in the end, this gives us a p sub

166
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:40,440
x times the summation of xi bar is

167
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:44,880
going to be equal to px times the summation of ai times

168
00:06:44,880 --> 00:06:52,520
xi bar plus py times the summation of ai yi bar.

169
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:55,200
By the way, if you're wondering what happened to the v's,

170
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:57,320
whenever you're wondering about where the v's went,

171
00:06:57,320 --> 00:07:00,200
I'm using that.

172
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:02,240
So I am using this assumption.

173
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:04,400
This, by the way, is not even an important assumption

174
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:07,000
because you could always normalize this equation so

175
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:08,200
that they do sum to 1.

176
00:07:08,200 --> 00:07:10,280
So this is not actually a substantive assumption.

177
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:12,280
That's something you can get for free.

178
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:15,960
So again, imagine that a plus b sum to 1.7.

179
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:18,480
We can just divide both sides of this equation by 1.7,

180
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:20,120
and then we're OK.

181
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:23,520
Then a plus b goes on to 1 again.

182
00:07:23,520 --> 00:07:25,520
And remember, what's the difference

183
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:27,840
in maximizing utility and maximizing utility divided

184
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:30,040
by 1.7?

185
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:31,680
No difference.

186
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:32,920
There's no difference at all.

187
00:07:32,920 --> 00:07:34,920
What's the difference between maximizing profits

188
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:36,000
and half of profits?

189
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:37,720
It's the same thing.

190
00:07:37,720 --> 00:07:39,640
The way you maximize half of profits

191
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:41,200
is for maximizing profits.

192
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:44,840
Same way you maximize twice, you've got two times profits.

193
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:47,240
Justice aside, this is one of the dumbest arguments

194
00:07:47,240 --> 00:07:51,240
for why excess profit taxation is harmless.

195
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:53,960
But I have seen economists make a straight face.

196
00:07:53,960 --> 00:07:55,600
Look, it's just calculus.

197
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:58,560
Excess profit taxation doesn't harm incentives at all

198
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:02,520
because if people are doing something, maximizing profits,

199
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:04,800
then we can take half away, and they still

200
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:06,600
do exactly the same thing.

201
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:08,400
I'll leave you to ponder why this actually

202
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:09,440
is such a dumb argument.

203
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:14,480
Back to this.

204
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:17,040
So then we can go and solve for this whole thing.

205
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:21,160
And what we will get is Px over Py.

206
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:24,680
So notice, there aren't any subscripts for individuals

207
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:26,120
for Px and Py.

208
00:08:26,120 --> 00:08:28,800
Why do the prices not have individual subscripts?

209
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:34,920
Because there's no price discrimination.

210
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:38,320
Because this is a market.

211
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:40,560
This is a market where each individual is not

212
00:08:40,560 --> 00:08:41,920
paying a price.

213
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:45,320
Instead, there is a common social price

214
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:48,000
that everyone can access.

215
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,760
And this is going to be equal to what?

216
00:08:50,760 --> 00:08:56,240
So it's going to be equal to summation of ai, yi bar,

217
00:08:56,240 --> 00:08:58,920
over summation of bi.

218
00:08:58,920 --> 00:09:02,320
And we have bi, xi, bar.

219
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:03,880
How do I get the b come back?

220
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:06,880
Well, I'll be back.

221
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:08,720
It's easier to do the math without it,

222
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:11,280
and then in the end, it looks cleaner if you put it back in.

223
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:13,720
So it's perfectly fine to substitute something out

224
00:09:13,720 --> 00:09:16,400
and substitute it back in at the end.

225
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:20,040
And once again, this makes total sense intuitively

226
00:09:20,040 --> 00:09:25,200
because this is saying that if one individual's intensity

227
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:29,120
or preference for x goes up, then that makes x more

228
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:30,040
expensive.

229
00:09:30,040 --> 00:09:31,880
And if the preference for y goes up,

230
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:33,640
then it makes x less expensive.

231
00:09:33,640 --> 00:09:35,720
And if the social stockpile of y goes up,

232
00:09:35,720 --> 00:09:37,360
then x becomes pricier.

233
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:41,800
And if the social stockpile of x goes up, then x becomes cheaper.

234
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:43,800
All right, now if you're paying close attention,

235
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:44,880
you say, wait a second.

236
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:46,380
You said if one individual changes,

237
00:09:46,380 --> 00:09:47,340
that changes the price?

238
00:09:47,340 --> 00:09:48,800
But isn't the whole basis of this

239
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:51,280
that no individual does change the price?

240
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:56,240
Yeah, there is actually a little contradiction here,

241
00:09:56,240 --> 00:09:59,880
because essentially what this means

242
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:02,920
is that in reality, as long as these

243
00:10:02,920 --> 00:10:05,680
are individuals of finite size, they actually

244
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:07,280
do have a very tiny effect.

245
00:10:07,280 --> 00:10:09,640
And yet we've reasoned all the way through assuming

246
00:10:09,640 --> 00:10:10,680
there is wrong about it.

247
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:12,800
So one way you can salvage this is say, yeah, well,

248
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:15,440
I'm just assuming that people just round down to zero

249
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:17,160
even incorrectly.

250
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:19,880
That would be one way of rescuing the model.

251
00:10:19,880 --> 00:10:21,720
The other thing you can do, which we're not

252
00:10:21,720 --> 00:10:24,480
going to suffer with, is fine, let's redo all the math,

253
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:26,800
but assume a continuum of agents.

254
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:30,520
So it's no longer one, say, a quadrillion agents.

255
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:34,680
Instead, it's going to be a continuum of human beings

256
00:10:34,680 --> 00:10:38,120
with a measure of one.

257
00:10:38,120 --> 00:10:41,520
And then each of them is an infinitesimal.

258
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:44,240
Every one individual is an infinitesimal amount.

259
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:45,800
And if you're an infinitesimal amount,

260
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:48,600
then you really don't have any effect on anything.

261
00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:51,280
So you could go either route.

262
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:53,680
Mathematicians will generally like the second route.

263
00:10:53,680 --> 00:10:55,920
We're fine with a switch to a totally continuous world

264
00:10:55,920 --> 00:11:00,800
where individuals are a continuum of individuals.

265
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:02,080
Is this really a thing?

266
00:11:02,080 --> 00:11:03,200
Yes, it's totally a thing.

267
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:05,040
There's theorists who spend their whole lives

268
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:07,840
working with continuum of agents.

269
00:11:07,840 --> 00:11:10,360
And then what about the other thing where we just say, yeah,

270
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:12,640
well, people think that they have no effect,

271
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:17,160
but actually they do have an ever so slight effect.

272
00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:18,000
You can do that too.

273
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,440
That would be very much in the spirit of Richard Thaler

274
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:24,240
for people to say, well, people look at the world,

275
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:27,200
and in fact, they have a 1 7 billionth influence.

276
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:28,880
But they just say, it's zero.

277
00:11:28,880 --> 00:11:29,960
Nobody cares about you.

278
00:11:29,960 --> 00:11:32,920
You're boring.

279
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:34,760
All right, now something else to point out

280
00:11:34,760 --> 00:11:37,120
is we get exactly the same results

281
00:11:37,120 --> 00:11:42,480
if instead of substituting out for y, we substitute out for x.

282
00:11:42,480 --> 00:11:45,360
So we could redo all the math with the other route

283
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:48,320
that we currently find.

284
00:11:48,320 --> 00:11:50,920
And then there is a nice intuition behind this,

285
00:11:50,920 --> 00:11:53,480
which is if we only have two markets and one of them

286
00:11:53,480 --> 00:11:56,920
is clearing, what must the other one be doing too?

287
00:11:56,920 --> 00:11:57,880
Clearing, right?

288
00:11:57,880 --> 00:11:59,480
Because it's a barter market.

289
00:11:59,480 --> 00:12:02,480
So if the x market is in equilibrium,

290
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:04,800
then there's no way that the y market

291
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:06,040
wouldn't be in equilibrium.

292
00:12:06,040 --> 00:12:08,360
The only way the x market is in equilibrium

293
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:11,760
is if at that price, there is neither excess supply

294
00:12:11,760 --> 00:12:14,240
nor excess demand of x.

295
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:18,080
And what it means for y to be out of equilibrium

296
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:21,400
is the flip side of that very thing.

297
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:24,880
So happy with that idea?

298
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:26,160
That's good.

299
00:12:26,160 --> 00:12:28,640
All right, now one thing that you

300
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:31,320
might notice about these utility functions

301
00:12:31,320 --> 00:12:35,480
is that they imply that every individual is,

302
00:12:35,480 --> 00:12:38,400
as long as a and b are both greater than zero,

303
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:40,360
every individual is absolutely desperate to have

304
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:42,840
a little bit of each good.

305
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:47,800
All right, so what would your utility be if you had zero y?

306
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:48,800
What's the log of zero?

307
00:12:51,800 --> 00:12:52,300
Negative.

308
00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:59,840
What's that really big number that isn't actually a number?

309
00:12:59,840 --> 00:13:00,840
Negative infinity.

310
00:13:00,840 --> 00:13:02,240
Negative infinity.

311
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:06,240
So no one wants utility negative infinity.

312
00:13:06,240 --> 00:13:10,080
Negative infinity is a really bad utility to have.

313
00:13:10,080 --> 00:13:16,240
So this means that if you were in danger of having zero y,

314
00:13:16,240 --> 00:13:18,560
you would give up virtually every scrap of x

315
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:23,960
you got just to avoid the horrible fate of having zero y.

316
00:13:23,960 --> 00:13:28,200
So why then, in a model where suppose some people just

317
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:31,680
started off with no y, suppose that for some people

318
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:35,040
y i bar were zero, why is it that these people don't just

319
00:13:35,040 --> 00:13:37,920
get expropriated by the markets?

320
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:41,640
Why is it that they still wind up getting to go and pay

321
00:13:41,640 --> 00:13:47,000
the same price as the people that start with plenty of y?

322
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:48,800
What's going on here?

323
00:13:48,800 --> 00:13:52,520
People act as if their actions are under effect.

324
00:13:52,520 --> 00:13:53,240
Right, right.

325
00:13:53,240 --> 00:13:57,640
But more intuitively, this is a competitive market.

326
00:13:57,640 --> 00:14:00,640
So if you had no y, you went to someone and said,

327
00:14:00,640 --> 00:14:01,800
I'd like to buy a little y.

328
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:04,080
And they said, sure, give me everything you have except

329
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:06,840
for one epsilon of x.

330
00:14:06,840 --> 00:14:10,840
And then you'd say, I think I'll go someplace else.

331
00:14:10,840 --> 00:14:13,520
So it would be like trying to buy water in the desert

332
00:14:13,520 --> 00:14:16,200
when there's 1,000 water stands right next to you.

333
00:14:16,200 --> 00:14:18,720
You desperately need water from somebody or other,

334
00:14:18,720 --> 00:14:19,840
or you'll die.

335
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:22,160
But it doesn't make you actually desperate for water

336
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:23,680
from any one individual.

337
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:26,480
It doesn't mean that you are going to pay any noticeable

338
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:29,360
price.

339
00:14:29,360 --> 00:14:32,160
This is a generalization of something

340
00:14:32,160 --> 00:14:34,720
that you may have seen at some point in undergraduate

341
00:14:34,720 --> 00:14:35,800
economics.

342
00:14:35,800 --> 00:14:39,200
Where you can suppose that a worker has a vertical labor

343
00:14:39,200 --> 00:14:40,600
supply curve.

344
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:42,560
And then there's labor demand.

345
00:14:42,560 --> 00:14:44,520
And then someone might say, well,

346
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:47,280
since he was with that vertical labor supply curve,

347
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:49,440
he'd be perfectly happy to go and work for peanuts down

348
00:14:49,440 --> 00:14:50,480
there, right?

349
00:14:50,480 --> 00:14:55,800
And then there are plenty of Marxist economists.

350
00:14:55,800 --> 00:14:57,680
So the one who said it actually most clearly

351
00:14:57,680 --> 00:15:00,280
was the anarcho-economist Maconin, who just said,

352
00:15:00,280 --> 00:15:02,320
well, sure, since the worker will take anything,

353
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:04,520
he will get next to nothing.

354
00:15:04,520 --> 00:15:07,680
And again, what is the error with this assumption?

355
00:15:07,680 --> 00:15:10,320
So think about it yourself.

356
00:15:10,320 --> 00:15:14,880
So suppose that you have no other source of income.

357
00:15:14,880 --> 00:15:17,080
And so the only thing you can do,

358
00:15:17,080 --> 00:15:19,040
the only way to get any money was to work.

359
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:20,680
And suppose that the only offer you had

360
00:15:20,680 --> 00:15:22,080
was for starvation wages.

361
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:23,320
Just keep alive.

362
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:25,400
Would you take that deal?

363
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:26,240
Probably.

364
00:15:26,240 --> 00:15:28,880
Does that mean you're going to get that deal?

365
00:15:28,880 --> 00:15:29,640
No, why not?

366
00:15:29,640 --> 00:15:32,200
What's going to happen if an employer says, hey,

367
00:15:32,200 --> 00:15:33,080
tell you what.

368
00:15:33,080 --> 00:15:36,440
I'll go and offer you $1 a day.

369
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:38,600
That's your offer.

370
00:15:38,600 --> 00:15:40,360
So we're right down here.

371
00:15:40,360 --> 00:15:41,240
So just look at this.

372
00:15:41,240 --> 00:15:42,640
So this is just supply and demand.

373
00:15:42,640 --> 00:15:43,720
This is not PhD micro.

374
00:15:43,720 --> 00:15:44,880
So supply and demand.

375
00:15:44,880 --> 00:15:48,640
So at that really low price, we've got an excess something.

376
00:15:48,640 --> 00:15:50,560
What's the excess?

377
00:15:50,560 --> 00:15:51,440
Excess demand, right?

378
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:54,520
Because at that really low price, people want a ton.

379
00:15:54,520 --> 00:15:56,560
But there's only that amount around.

380
00:15:56,560 --> 00:15:59,560
And so if you were offered that amount,

381
00:15:59,560 --> 00:16:02,040
what would other employers be eager to do?

382
00:16:02,040 --> 00:16:05,480
Offer you a raise.

383
00:16:05,480 --> 00:16:07,560
And if you think about this a little more,

384
00:16:07,560 --> 00:16:10,000
I say, well, look, this holds just as well for Tom Cruise

385
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:11,800
as it does for a dish digger.

386
00:16:11,800 --> 00:16:13,200
Does it even worry the Tom Cruise?

387
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:17,000
Since the entire movie industry conspired

388
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,080
to offer him only $1.

389
00:16:19,080 --> 00:16:21,400
I guess this is back before he had any money of his own.

390
00:16:21,400 --> 00:16:24,120
If they all conspired to offer him only $1 a day for his acting,

391
00:16:24,120 --> 00:16:26,440
they'd probably find all this said to act for $1 a day.

392
00:16:26,440 --> 00:16:27,240
And I'll be hungry.

393
00:16:27,240 --> 00:16:31,000
I'll play one of the really thin roles.

394
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,440
I'll play one of Christian Bale's many starvation roles.

395
00:16:34,440 --> 00:16:35,800
There's a guy I don't understand.

396
00:16:35,800 --> 00:16:38,840
You're that rich and you starve yourself not just once.

397
00:16:38,840 --> 00:16:40,920
He starved himself several times, actually.

398
00:16:40,920 --> 00:16:41,840
Three different roles.

399
00:16:41,840 --> 00:16:44,400
He's gone really hungry.

400
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:47,800
He went down to 90 pounds for The Machinist.

401
00:16:47,800 --> 00:16:48,920
That's not special effects.

402
00:16:48,920 --> 00:16:52,960
You see this movie, it's like, that's Batman?

403
00:16:52,960 --> 00:16:55,400
For Batman, he was going to get, what's that?

404
00:16:55,400 --> 00:16:56,440
Right before Batman.

405
00:16:56,440 --> 00:16:57,520
Then he had to gain.

406
00:16:57,520 --> 00:16:58,040
Yeah, yeah.

407
00:16:58,040 --> 00:17:03,120
So he more than doubled his weight between roles.

408
00:17:03,120 --> 00:17:06,120
He ate an apple and a cup of coffee each day on The Machinist.

409
00:17:06,120 --> 00:17:08,640
And if you see him, you're just like, oh my god.

410
00:17:08,640 --> 00:17:10,320
I can't believe he survived that.

411
00:17:10,320 --> 00:17:13,440
Anyway, that would be what he would be able to afford

412
00:17:13,440 --> 00:17:14,560
at that wage.

413
00:17:14,560 --> 00:17:16,520
But again, as long as there's competition,

414
00:17:16,520 --> 00:17:18,360
no one is worried that Christian Bale actually

415
00:17:18,360 --> 00:17:19,560
is going to starve.

416
00:17:19,560 --> 00:17:23,480
Because since he produces something of so much value,

417
00:17:23,480 --> 00:17:24,400
there's competition.

418
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:25,800
And that's what's going to happen.

419
00:17:25,800 --> 00:17:29,200
And it's the same thing over here.

420
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:30,040
All right.

421
00:17:30,040 --> 00:17:32,880
So questions about anything we've done so far?

422
00:17:32,880 --> 00:17:34,680
I'm happy with general equilibrium.

423
00:17:34,680 --> 00:17:36,840
Everyone understand how we've done something better

424
00:17:36,840 --> 00:17:38,880
than we did in intermediate micro?

425
00:17:38,880 --> 00:17:41,640
What we've done better is we have deduced

426
00:17:41,640 --> 00:17:44,160
how a whole society behaves from assumptions

427
00:17:44,160 --> 00:17:46,480
about the individuals that comprise it.

428
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:49,120
Whereas in intermediate micro, the most you're ever

429
00:17:49,120 --> 00:17:50,600
likely to have done is figure out

430
00:17:50,600 --> 00:17:54,800
what happens for one maximized individual, which

431
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:58,080
is a much narrower question.

432
00:17:58,080 --> 00:17:59,540
So now we're going to go and think

433
00:17:59,540 --> 00:18:02,760
about general equilibrium in pure exchange economies.

434
00:18:02,760 --> 00:18:04,680
So now we're going to go and try to analyze

435
00:18:04,680 --> 00:18:06,640
them in very general terms.

436
00:18:06,640 --> 00:18:07,320
All right.

437
00:18:07,320 --> 00:18:09,960
So now we're going to stick with our assumption

438
00:18:09,960 --> 00:18:13,160
that we've got i consumers where i is any number.

439
00:18:13,160 --> 00:18:15,640
But now we're going to go away from two commodities

440
00:18:15,640 --> 00:18:18,040
all the way up to an arbitrary number of commodities.

441
00:18:18,040 --> 00:18:20,800
So now we can have k commodities where k is any number,

442
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:22,680
any number you want.

443
00:18:22,680 --> 00:18:25,040
And then we need to add on a few other assumptions

444
00:18:25,040 --> 00:18:26,800
just to keep everything clean.

445
00:18:26,800 --> 00:18:28,560
So most of these assumptions are just

446
00:18:28,560 --> 00:18:29,760
mathematical housekeeping.

447
00:18:29,760 --> 00:18:31,200
They're not things that would even

448
00:18:31,200 --> 00:18:33,400
occur to someone to dispute.

449
00:18:33,400 --> 00:18:35,640
So commodity consumption must be non-negative.

450
00:18:35,640 --> 00:18:37,760
You can't consume negative p's.

451
00:18:37,760 --> 00:18:39,320
For example, has anyone ever consumed

452
00:18:39,320 --> 00:18:41,120
a negative amount of p's?

453
00:18:41,120 --> 00:18:42,080
All right, good.

454
00:18:42,080 --> 00:18:42,580
All right.

455
00:18:42,580 --> 00:18:43,920
So then that's OK.

456
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:45,040
All right, next assumption.

457
00:18:45,040 --> 00:18:48,320
So assume that utility of x is strictly

458
00:18:48,320 --> 00:18:50,240
increasing in all commodities.

459
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:53,280
This is actually stronger than necessary.

460
00:18:53,280 --> 00:18:55,400
One of the games that mathematical economists play

461
00:18:55,400 --> 00:18:57,480
is what is the weakest assumption that

462
00:18:57,480 --> 00:19:00,400
guarantees absolute certainty.

463
00:19:00,400 --> 00:19:02,400
And if you're really curious, it turns out

464
00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:04,820
the weakest assumption that guarantees absolute certainty

465
00:19:04,820 --> 00:19:06,760
is called local non-satiation.

466
00:19:06,760 --> 00:19:08,720
If this sounds really boring to you,

467
00:19:08,720 --> 00:19:10,760
then you'll never need to know it again.

468
00:19:10,760 --> 00:19:13,800
But if you're curious, then you can go and Google that.

469
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:15,240
You can read the Wikipedia article

470
00:19:15,240 --> 00:19:17,400
on local non-satiation.

471
00:19:17,400 --> 00:19:18,360
All right.

472
00:19:18,360 --> 00:19:21,040
Then consumers start with endowment of commodities.

473
00:19:21,040 --> 00:19:22,800
So endowment of consumer i is going

474
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:27,720
to be indicated by e super i.

475
00:19:27,720 --> 00:19:31,080
And now e is actually a vector.

476
00:19:31,080 --> 00:19:32,440
Remember what is a vector?

477
00:19:32,440 --> 00:19:34,920
It's a bunch of numbers held as a package.

478
00:19:34,920 --> 00:19:43,880
So your endowment vector could be three pigs, two pancakes,

479
00:19:43,880 --> 00:19:54,560
one pound of cheese, and then three tic-tacs.

480
00:19:54,560 --> 00:19:55,400
So that's a vector.

481
00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:58,440
So that is a four-good vector.

482
00:19:58,440 --> 00:20:14,560
So anything strange?


